{-
   Exclusivity across control flow - the half of `Reject.Ownership.yana` that a single block cannot
   state.

   That file's `swap(value, value)` is two mutable borrows in one expression, and it was the whole of
   what this rule was tested against. Everything below is instead a borrow whose extent *spans*
   blocks, which is where the question stops being "are these two lines next to each other" and
   becomes "is the borrow live at this write" - and those are different questions in both directions:

    - a write inside a loop the borrow is held across is a conflict, though the loop body may be
      numbered anywhere at all relative to the borrow;
    - a write in a loop the borrow was taken *after* is not, though the numbering puts the loop body
      later - see BorrowExtent in analyze_borrow.cpp, and `LiveAfterLoop.yana` for the accepting side;
    - a write on the arm a borrow was not taken on is not, though both arms lie between the borrow
      and its use in any linear reading of the function.

   Every case here is a *rejection*, and the accepting cases are `LiveAfterLoop.yana` beside it. The
   two files are one property seen from its two sides, and a check that answered "conflict" to
   everything would pass this one alone.

   `pick` is what makes a borrow outlive the statement that took it: it hands one of its arguments
   back, so the loan on both survives to the last use of the result. That is Design.md's "the caller
   conservatively keeps every member borrowed until the last use of all result borrows", and it is
   the only way to write a long-lived borrow in the language today.
-}

fn pick(return &a: I64, return &b: I64, first: Bool) -> &I64 = if first then a else b

{-
   A borrow taken before a loop and used after it, so it is live across every iteration.

   The write is in the loop body, which reverse postorder numbers *after* the block holding the
   borrow - the same shape that used to be reported when the borrow was not live there at all. Here
   it genuinely is, and this is the case that says so.
-}
fn acrossLoop(&x: I64, &y: I64) -> I64:
    let &chosen = pick(x, y, True)
    let &i = 0 :: I64

    while i < 4:
        x = x + 1
        i = i + 1

    return chosen

-- The same across one arm of a branch: the borrow is live on both paths out of the `if`, so the arm
-- that writes is a conflict whichever way control went.
fn acrossBranch(&x: I64, &y: I64, flag: Bool) -> I64:
    let &chosen = pick(x, y, True)
    if flag then x = 7

    return chosen

{-
   And through a merge, which is the case the extent used to stop dead at.

   `chosen` is a phi of two borrows and may name either, so a use of it keeps *both* alive - and the
   write after the merge conflicts with both, which is why this reports twice. Following the loan
   through the phi is what finds it; without that, nothing after the merge was reachable from either
   borrow and the write was accepted in silence.
-}
fn throughMerge(&x: I64, &y: I64, flag: Bool) -> I64:
    let &chosen = if flag then pick(x, y, True) else pick(y, x, True)
    x = x + 1

    return chosen
